    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a house in oregon. gotta use a big douglas fir beam for the main span. the span is gonna be about 14 feet. i'm worried about some of the knots and potential internal check/splits in the timber i'm looking at. what should i be looking out for in terms of load bearing capacity if the wood isn't perfectly clear?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Douglas fir beams with knots might have reduced load-bearing capacity. Be wary of large knots, splits (internal check/heart shake), and changes in grain direction, as they can compromise structural integrity. For your 14-foot span, ensure the timber follows the local building codes and consider consulting with a structural engineer to evaluate its suitability for your project. Shear and bearing strength should also be checked, using calculations or structural analysis to confirm safe loads can be supported by the beam. If in doubt, select a clearer, more uniform timber for stronger and more predictable performance.",
